  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec\u2061x?$\n\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is in the domain.\n- $x=\\pi $ is a root. $$\n- $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is not in the range of the function.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ thus it is not defined for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has no zeros.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ does not belong to the range.",
